1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,160
Imagine you buy the safest crypto wallet

2
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:03,220
money can buy.

3
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:04,740
A little device, sealed in its box,

4
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:05,960
built to do one thing.

5
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:07,300
Keep your secret safe.

6
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:08,320
You set it up.

7
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,940
It shows you twenty four words.

8
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:11,120
You write them down on paper,

9
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,480
the way you're supposed to.

10
00:00:12,536 --> 00:00:14,236
Three weeks later, every coin you put on

11
00:00:14,256 --> 00:00:15,056
it is gone.

12
00:00:15,076 --> 00:00:16,496
You never typed those words anywhere.

13
00:00:16,516 --> 00:00:17,856
You never clicked a bad link.

14
00:00:17,998 --> 00:00:19,873
Because the thief wasn't on your computer.

15
00:00:19,893 --> 00:00:21,498
The thief came in the box.

16
00:00:21,518 --> 00:00:22,581
And the worst part?

17
00:00:22,601 --> 00:00:23,664
You did everything right.

18
00:00:23,684 --> 00:00:24,747
You bought the safe one.

19
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:26,388
You followed every rule.

20
00:00:26,421 --> 00:00:27,521
I'm Vio.

21
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:29,121
And this is the story of the spy chip that

22
00:00:29,141 --> 00:00:30,621
drained more than ninety million dollars

23
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:32,421
from hundreds of crypto wallets.

24
00:00:32,621 --> 00:00:34,241
And the investigation is still going on

25
00:00:34,261 --> 00:00:35,261
right now.

26
00:00:35,462 --> 00:00:37,182
To understand how scary this is,

27
00:00:37,202 --> 00:00:39,162
you need to know one thing about these wallets.

28
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:41,750
Those twenty four words are not a password.

29
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:42,910
They are the money.

30
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:44,350
Anyone who has them can rebuild your

31
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:46,090
wallet on any device, anywhere in the

32
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:47,950
world, and move everything out.

33
00:00:48,138 --> 00:00:50,598
That's why a hardware wallet like a Ledger exists.

34
00:00:50,798 --> 00:00:52,118
It creates the words inside a special

35
00:00:52,138 --> 00:00:53,898
chip, shows them once on its own little

36
00:00:53,918 --> 00:00:55,838
screen, and never sends them anywhere.

37
00:00:56,098 --> 00:00:56,998
Not to your laptop.

38
00:00:57,018 --> 00:00:57,958
Not to the internet.

39
00:00:58,138 --> 00:00:58,818
Nowhere.

40
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:00,514
And to prove a device is real,

41
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:01,574
Ledger runs a check.

42
00:01:01,834 --> 00:01:03,814
If the secure chip inside is genuine,

43
00:01:03,834 --> 00:01:04,914
the device passes.

44
00:01:05,214 --> 00:01:07,354
But that check only looks at the secure chip.

45
00:01:07,374 --> 00:01:09,314
Remember that, because it's about to matter.

46
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,696
So how do you steal from a box that is

47
00:01:11,716 --> 00:01:13,014
built to never leak a secret?

48
00:01:13,034 --> 00:01:13,673
You don't hack it.

49
00:01:13,693 --> 00:01:15,290
You add something to it.

50
00:01:15,411 --> 00:01:17,111
On October thirteenth, Ledger's own

51
00:01:17,131 --> 00:01:18,691
security team confirmed what they had

52
00:01:18,711 --> 00:01:20,571
found inside some of its devices.

53
00:01:20,904 --> 00:01:22,384
A second, hidden component.

54
00:01:22,404 --> 00:01:23,884
It wasn't supposed to be there.

55
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:25,744
And it wasn't touching the secure chip at all.

56
00:01:25,764 --> 00:01:27,104
It was watching the screen.

57
00:01:27,332 --> 00:01:28,792
When the owner set up the device and the

58
00:01:28,812 --> 00:01:30,232
twenty four words appeared,

59
00:01:30,252 --> 00:01:31,732
the implant reportedly read them right off

60
00:01:31,752 --> 00:01:32,692
the display.

61
00:01:32,914 --> 00:01:34,074
And then it sent them out.

62
00:01:34,094 --> 00:01:35,154
Not through your Wi-Fi.

63
00:01:35,174 --> 00:01:36,254
Not through your computer.

64
00:01:36,274 --> 00:01:37,814
The chip had its own mobile connection,

65
00:01:37,834 --> 00:01:39,314
with a built-in digital SIM card.

66
00:01:39,334 --> 00:01:41,414
Like a tiny phone, hidden inside your wallet.

67
00:01:41,454 --> 00:01:43,094
Think about how clever that is.

68
00:01:43,274 --> 00:01:44,734
Most careful crypto owners keep their

69
00:01:44,754 --> 00:01:46,214
wallet away from the internet.

70
00:01:46,334 --> 00:01:47,454
That's the whole idea.

71
00:01:47,494 --> 00:01:49,394
This one brought its own internet with it.

72
00:01:49,486 --> 00:01:51,426
And here's the part that made it almost invisible.

73
00:01:51,786 --> 00:01:53,306
Because the secure chip was untouched,

74
00:01:53,366 --> 00:01:54,946
Ledger says these devices could still pass

75
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:56,346
its authenticity check.

76
00:01:56,366 --> 00:01:57,746
The box said genuine.

77
00:01:57,866 --> 00:01:58,826
And it was lying.

78
00:01:59,038 --> 00:02:00,618
And the attackers didn't empty the wallets

79
00:02:00,638 --> 00:02:02,218
the moment they got the words.

80
00:02:02,478 --> 00:02:03,438
It looks like they waited,

81
00:02:03,458 --> 00:02:04,718
while people kept adding money,

82
00:02:04,738 --> 00:02:05,878
week after week.

83
00:02:06,008 --> 00:02:08,648
Then, on October ninth, they pulled the trigger.

84
00:02:08,903 --> 00:02:10,263
Wallet after wallet emptied,

85
00:02:10,283 --> 00:02:11,703
all in the same short window.

86
00:02:11,723 --> 00:02:13,123
On-chain investigators who traced the

87
00:02:13,143 --> 00:02:14,463
money say more than three hundred wallets

88
00:02:14,483 --> 00:02:15,383
were hit.

89
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:16,816
Their estimates started around seventy

90
00:02:16,836 --> 00:02:18,016
million dollars.

91
00:02:18,036 --> 00:02:19,196
Then eighty six.

92
00:02:19,216 --> 00:02:20,196
Then ninety three.

93
00:02:20,216 --> 00:02:22,216
Some now say over a hundred million.

94
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:24,806
Ledger says it hasn't verified those numbers.

95
00:02:24,986 --> 00:02:26,906
But nobody is arguing that it's small.

96
00:02:27,023 --> 00:02:29,083
Now imagine being one of those owners.

97
00:02:29,303 --> 00:02:31,063
You open your app on a normal Friday.

98
00:02:31,083 --> 00:02:32,243
Your balance says zero.

99
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:33,743
The device is still on your desk.

100
00:02:33,763 --> 00:02:35,003
Still sealed in your drawer.

101
00:02:35,023 --> 00:02:37,143
And you have no idea how anyone got in.

102
00:02:37,232 --> 00:02:39,052
Tether, the company behind the USDT

103
00:02:39,072 --> 00:02:41,032
stablecoin, moved to freeze some of the

104
00:02:41,052 --> 00:02:42,032
stolen coins.

105
00:02:42,272 --> 00:02:43,832
And part of the rest was reportedly pushed

106
00:02:43,852 --> 00:02:46,152
through a crypto mixer, to hide where it went.

107
00:02:46,532 --> 00:02:47,872
One on-chain tracker reported that the

108
00:02:47,892 --> 00:02:49,352
attacker pushed about four hundred and

109
00:02:49,372 --> 00:02:51,412
thirty ether, roughly a million dollars,

110
00:02:51,432 --> 00:02:53,212
through Tornado Cash, a service built to

111
00:02:53,232 --> 00:02:54,732
make crypto hard to follow.

112
00:02:54,859 --> 00:02:56,339
And then the blockchain analysis firm

113
00:02:56,359 --> 00:02:58,899
Chainalysis found something almost funny.

114
00:02:59,028 --> 00:03:00,308
One of the wallets that got drained

115
00:03:00,328 --> 00:03:01,848
belonged to a scammer.

116
00:03:01,868 --> 00:03:03,188
Someone who had been running a fake job

117
00:03:03,208 --> 00:03:05,728
scam, paying people to post fake reviews,

118
00:03:05,748 --> 00:03:07,508
then demanding deposits from them.

119
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,920
On September twenty second,

120
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:10,720
that scammer moved their stolen money onto

121
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:12,200
three Ledger addresses.

122
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:13,380
To keep it safe.

123
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,000
On October ninth, all three were emptied

124
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:16,740
into the same thief's wallet.

125
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:17,980
Over sixty bitcoin.

126
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:20,020
About four point eight million dollars.

127
00:03:20,216 --> 00:03:21,716
Thieves, robbing a thief,

128
00:03:21,736 --> 00:03:22,716
with a spy chip.

129
00:03:22,968 --> 00:03:25,268
So where did these devices come from?

130
00:03:25,314 --> 00:03:27,294
Ledger says every confirmed loss so far

131
00:03:27,314 --> 00:03:29,974
involves devices sold through one reseller.

132
00:03:29,994 --> 00:03:32,754
A company in Southeast Asia called CryptoBilis.

133
00:03:32,896 --> 00:03:34,336
Not Ledger's own store.

134
00:03:34,356 --> 00:03:35,036
A middleman.

135
00:03:35,056 --> 00:03:36,536
And that's exactly the weak spot.

136
00:03:36,596 --> 00:03:37,756
Somewhere between the factory and the

137
00:03:37,776 --> 00:03:39,476
buyer, someone opened these boxes,

138
00:03:39,496 --> 00:03:41,436
added the implant, and sealed them again.

139
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:43,400
Think of it like a sealed bottle of water.

140
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:45,180
You trust it because the cap is closed.

141
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:46,340
But if someone opened it,

142
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:47,760
changed what's inside, and closed it again

143
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,240
perfectly, you would never know.

144
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:50,360
You would just drink it.

145
00:03:50,401 --> 00:03:52,001
Who did it, and where in that chain it

146
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:54,141
happened, is still being investigated.

147
00:03:54,318 --> 00:03:56,558
Ledger stopped sales in Southeast Asia and

148
00:03:56,578 --> 00:03:58,758
tightened how its devices are stored and shipped.

149
00:03:58,898 --> 00:04:00,258
Trezor, another wallet maker,

150
00:04:00,278 --> 00:04:01,658
said it found no tampering in its own

151
00:04:01,678 --> 00:04:03,898
devices, but still dropped CryptoBilis as

152
00:04:03,918 --> 00:04:04,658
a seller.

153
00:04:05,038 --> 00:04:06,658
OneKey offered free replacements.

154
00:04:06,898 --> 00:04:08,718
Ledger also stopped returned devices from

155
00:04:08,738 --> 00:04:09,738
being resold.

156
00:04:10,278 --> 00:04:11,778
Because any box that goes back to a shop

157
00:04:11,798 --> 00:04:13,838
can be opened, changed, and sold again to

158
00:04:13,858 --> 00:04:14,798
someone else.

159
00:04:14,987 --> 00:04:16,327
And if the story ended there,

160
00:04:16,347 --> 00:04:18,047
it would be a problem in one region.

161
00:04:18,127 --> 00:04:20,047
But then a report came in from Germany.

162
00:04:20,375 --> 00:04:22,315
A customer in Germany reportedly found a

163
00:04:22,335 --> 00:04:23,495
tampered Ledger.

164
00:04:23,575 --> 00:04:24,555
One that had been bought in a regular

165
00:04:24,575 --> 00:04:26,155
store, in Europe.

166
00:04:26,336 --> 00:04:28,096
That claim hasn't been confirmed.

167
00:04:28,456 --> 00:04:29,456
But it was enough.

168
00:04:29,656 --> 00:04:31,356
MediaMarkt and Saturn, two of the biggest

169
00:04:31,376 --> 00:04:32,776
electronics chains in Europe,

170
00:04:32,796 --> 00:04:34,216
stopped selling Ledger devices.

171
00:04:34,614 --> 00:04:36,394
Ledger says it is reviewing reports of

172
00:04:36,414 --> 00:04:38,294
implants in other places too.

173
00:04:38,314 --> 00:04:40,454
Which means, right now, nobody can say for

174
00:04:40,474 --> 00:04:41,994
sure how far this goes.

175
00:04:42,241 --> 00:04:43,961
Think about what that does to people.

176
00:04:44,101 --> 00:04:45,481
The whole point of a hardware wallet is

177
00:04:45,501 --> 00:04:47,041
that you don't have to trust anyone.

178
00:04:47,221 --> 00:04:48,921
And suddenly, you have to trust every hand

179
00:04:48,941 --> 00:04:50,361
the box passed through.

180
00:04:50,381 --> 00:04:51,881
And that's the real damage here.

181
00:04:51,901 --> 00:04:53,541
Not just the ninety million dollars.

182
00:04:53,561 --> 00:04:54,121
Trust.

183
00:04:54,281 --> 00:04:55,901
People keep their savings on a device the

184
00:04:55,921 --> 00:04:57,381
size of a USB stick.

185
00:04:57,525 --> 00:04:58,805
And quick favour.

186
00:04:58,985 --> 00:05:00,285
If you want more stories like this,

187
00:05:00,305 --> 00:05:01,265
where the danger is hiding inside

188
00:05:01,285 --> 00:05:03,225
something you trust, hit subscribe.

189
00:05:03,365 --> 00:05:05,365
It really helps a small channel like this one.

190
00:05:05,559 --> 00:05:07,439
Then the fight went public.

191
00:05:07,587 --> 00:05:09,407
On October seventeenth, Mark Karpelès

192
00:05:09,427 --> 00:05:10,747
jumped in on X.

193
00:05:10,967 --> 00:05:12,387
If that name sounds familiar,

194
00:05:12,407 --> 00:05:14,027
he ran Mount Gox, the bitcoin exchange

195
00:05:14,047 --> 00:05:15,767
that collapsed in twenty fourteen after

196
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:17,867
losing hundreds of thousands of bitcoin.

197
00:05:18,172 --> 00:05:19,772
Karpelès said Ledger had a guide about

198
00:05:19,792 --> 00:05:21,332
tampered devices since March,

199
00:05:21,352 --> 00:05:22,952
but it was buried in an education page and

200
00:05:22,972 --> 00:05:24,092
far too mild.

201
00:05:24,172 --> 00:05:25,332
He claimed a louder warning could have

202
00:05:25,352 --> 00:05:26,372
saved people.

203
00:05:26,491 --> 00:05:28,411
Ledger's CEO, Pascal Gauthier,

204
00:05:28,431 --> 00:05:29,251
hit back.

205
00:05:29,311 --> 00:05:31,151
He said Ledger works hard to protect its

206
00:05:31,171 --> 00:05:32,991
customers, and that a warning page can't

207
00:05:33,011 --> 00:05:34,351
stop a device that was tampered with

208
00:05:34,371 --> 00:05:36,191
before it ever reached the buyer.

209
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:37,580
And here's a detail that makes the

210
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:39,000
argument even messier.

211
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:40,780
That guide was first published in March,

212
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,300
but it was updated in September.

213
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:44,060
So right now, it's not even clear what the

214
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:45,440
original version said.

215
00:05:45,574 --> 00:05:46,954
And then he made it personal.

216
00:05:46,974 --> 00:05:49,814
He told Karpelès, quote, you are no white knight.

217
00:05:49,834 --> 00:05:50,694
Get real.

218
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:52,870
Even the founder of Binance weighed in.

219
00:05:52,890 --> 00:05:54,190
He still called Ledger one of the most

220
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:55,830
secure hardware wallets, but said a small

221
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:57,530
number of people probably bought fake or

222
00:05:57,550 --> 00:05:58,810
tampered Ledgers.

223
00:05:58,894 --> 00:06:00,314
And this isn't the first time Ledger

224
00:06:00,334 --> 00:06:02,014
owners were targeted through the mail.

225
00:06:02,234 --> 00:06:03,934
Back in twenty twenty, a leak exposed

226
00:06:03,954 --> 00:06:05,854
customers' names and home addresses.

227
00:06:06,214 --> 00:06:07,834
The year after, some of them received fake

228
00:06:07,854 --> 00:06:09,074
Ledger devices in the post,

229
00:06:09,094 --> 00:06:11,214
with a letter pretending to be from the CEO.

230
00:06:11,517 --> 00:06:13,877
So what should you actually do?

231
00:06:14,072 --> 00:06:15,592
Buy a hardware wallet only from the maker

232
00:06:15,612 --> 00:06:18,512
itself, or from a store the maker lists as official.

233
00:06:18,732 --> 00:06:20,292
A cheaper price from a random seller is

234
00:06:20,312 --> 00:06:21,192
not a deal.

235
00:06:21,212 --> 00:06:22,032
It's a risk.

236
00:06:22,212 --> 00:06:23,672
If a price looks too good to be true,

237
00:06:23,692 --> 00:06:25,132
ask yourself what else might be different

238
00:06:25,152 --> 00:06:26,312
about that box.

239
00:06:26,582 --> 00:06:28,062
Never use a device that arrives already

240
00:06:28,082 --> 00:06:29,822
set up, or with words already written down

241
00:06:29,842 --> 00:06:30,562
for you.

242
00:06:30,582 --> 00:06:32,002
Your twenty four words should be created

243
00:06:32,022 --> 00:06:33,482
by the device, in front of you,

244
00:06:33,502 --> 00:06:35,222
and nobody else should ever see them.

245
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:36,420
And if you bought a Ledger from

246
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:38,580
CryptoBilis, Ledger's advice is clear.

247
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,280
Don't set up an unused one.

248
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:41,920
If you already use one, move your money to

249
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:44,040
a new device with a brand new set of words.

250
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:45,820
Typing the old words into a new device

251
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:47,320
doesn't help, because the thief already

252
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:48,340
has them.

253
00:06:48,414 --> 00:06:50,154
And remember the bigger lesson.

254
00:06:50,494 --> 00:06:51,874
Every device you own travelled through a

255
00:06:51,894 --> 00:06:53,694
chain of hands before it reached you.

256
00:06:53,714 --> 00:06:54,934
Factories, warehouses,

257
00:06:55,094 --> 00:06:56,734
resellers, delivery drivers.

258
00:06:57,134 --> 00:06:58,814
Security isn't just about the device.

259
00:06:58,834 --> 00:07:00,954
It's about every step it took to get to you.

260
00:07:01,016 --> 00:07:02,676
So here's my question.

261
00:07:02,716 --> 00:07:04,136
Who should pay these people back?

262
00:07:04,156 --> 00:07:05,816
Ledger, the reseller, or nobody,

263
00:07:05,836 --> 00:07:07,536
because the buyers chose the cheaper seller?

264
00:07:07,556 --> 00:07:08,636
Tell me in the comments.

265
00:07:08,929 --> 00:07:11,009
And subscribe, because next time,

266
00:07:11,029 --> 00:07:12,469
there's another hack where the danger was

267
00:07:12,489 --> 00:07:14,749
hiding inside something people trusted.

268
00:07:14,875 --> 00:07:16,875
The safest box in crypto did exactly what

269
00:07:16,895 --> 00:07:17,695
it promised.

270
00:07:17,715 --> 00:07:19,055
It kept the secret safe.

271
00:07:19,075 --> 00:07:21,195
It just wasn't the only thing inside the box.
